.TH PHON 2
.SH NAME
phon \- English words in a voice model's own sounds
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "phon.m";
phon := load Phon Phon->PATH;

LEX: con "/lib/ml/en_US.lex";

init:  fn(path: string): string;
word:  fn(w: string): (string, string);
text:  fn(s: string): (string, string);
words: fn(): list of string;
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Phon
turns English into the phoneme symbols a voice model was trained on, so that
.IR say (1)
can be given words instead of sounds.
.PP
.B Init
reads a lexicon and returns nil, or why it could not.
A
.I path
of nil reads
.BR LEX .
The file is a word, a tab, and the sounds, one line per word;
.B #
begins a comment.
A word listed twice is an error rather than a silent choice between the two.
.PP
.B Word
looks up one word, already stripped of punctuation.
It returns the sounds, or nil and the word that is not known.
.B Text
does a whole line: it separates words, ignores case, keeps the punctuation the
voice was trained with because that is how a line is paced, and drops anything
else rather than sending a model a symbol it has never seen.
It returns the sounds, or nil and the
.I first
word it does not know, so that an author fixes them in the order they appear.
.PP
.B Words
returns every word in the lexicon, sorted.
.SH "TWO FILES"
.B Init
reads two things.
.B LEX
is hand-written, small, and read into memory;
.B DICT
is generated, about a million words, and searched where it lies.
The hand-written entries win, and that is the point of having both:
.I espeak-ng
gives
.B a
as the letter,
.BR ˈeɪ ,
where an article is almost always meant, and the only way to say so is to write
it down somewhere that outranks the generated file.
.PP
The dictionary is built by
.B /lib/ml/mklexicon.py
from the system word list, using
.I espeak-ng
on the host.
That is a build-time dependency and never a run-time one.
.I Espeak-ng
is the right source rather than a convenient one: this voice's
.B phoneme_type
is
.BR espeak ,
so the transcriptions land in exactly the symbol set the model was trained on,
and not one of the million entries used a symbol the voice does not know.
.PP
It is not read in.
A million sorted lines are binary-searched on disk by seeking, so the
dictionary costs no memory and no load time \(em two hundred lookups are not
measurable against the cost of starting the program that asks for them.
That is what makes it usable on a machine with no memory to spare, and it is
why
.B mklexicon.py
must keep emitting sorted output.
.SH "IT IS A LOOKUP"
There are no letter-to-sound rules here.
English spelling does not reduce to rules; a rule set that is right four times
in five gives a narrator that is wrong every fifth word, and
.I nothing in this system can hear that it happened .
A wrong pronunciation passes every check a program can make.
.PP
That argument is about narration, where the words are chosen in advance and a
refusal costs one line in a file.
It does not settle the question for reading a screen, where the words arrive
unbidden and a refusal is silence: there, rules under the dictionary are the
standard arrangement and the right one, because approximate beats absent.
This does not have them yet.
.PP
So today a word that is not in either file is an error naming the word, which someone
fixes by adding a line.
That is the whole design.
The alternative is not "a guess instead of an error" but "a guess instead of a
correction", and the guess is silent.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/lib/phon.b
.br
.B /lib/ml/en_US.lex
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR say (1),
.IR phontest (1),
.IR ml (3)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Every call returns a string saying what went wrong rather than raising.
.B Text
and
.B word
return the offending word itself as the error, not a sentence about it, so a
caller can say where to add it.
.SH BUGS
A word in neither file is refused, and for reading a screen that is the wrong
answer: filenames, identifiers, proper nouns and misspellings all fail, and
there are always more of them.
A dictionary cannot fix that \(em only letter-to-sound rules can, which is a
thing this does not have and eventually needs.
For narration, where the words are chosen in advance, refusing is right.
.PP
Inflections are generated by rule from a word list that has none, and where a
final consonant may or may not double, both spellings are emitted because the
letters do not say which \(em
.B stop
gives
.B stopped
and
.B open
gives
.BR opened .
The unused spelling costs space and is never looked up.
The rules do not cover irregular forms at all:
.B went
is in the dictionary only because it is its own entry in the word list.
.PP
The hand-written lexicon is small and hand-authored.
.IR phontest (1)
checks that every symbol in it is one the voice knows, and that five phrases
which had been listened to come back exactly \(em but nothing anywhere checks
how any other entry
.I sounds ,
and nothing can.
The same is true of the generated million, with the difference that they at
least come from a synthesizer that was listened to by somebody.
An entry that is wrong will be said wrong, and the file is where to fix it.
.PP
One pronunciation per word.
English has words whose sound depends on their part of speech \(em
.B read
and
.B read
are the same five letters \(em and this cannot express that.
.PP
Numbers are not spelled out.
.B 1993
is looked up as a word, is not in the lexicon, and is refused.
